      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Adeno-associated viral vectors have become widely used in the clinic for retinal gene therapy. Thanks to AAVs impeccable safety profile and positive functional outcomes in its clinical application, interest in retinal gene therapy has increased exponentially over the past decade. Although early clinical trials have shown there is little influence of neutralizing antibodies on the performance of AAV when vector is administered into the subretinal space, recent findings suggest neutralizing antibodies may play a role when AAV is delivered via the intravitreal route. These findings highlight the importance of microenvironment on gene delivery and stress the need for a versatile assay to screen subjects for the presence of AAV-neutralizing antibodies. Measuring NAb titers against AAV prior and after gene therapy will help us better understand the impact of preexisting immunity on gene transfer, especially when the vector is administered intravitreally."},
